A||||7||theogre||Service Warning, "Dog Bone",  4 cyl, 87+ mainly.||||1
Z||000000||theogre||10-11-1999||04:04 PM||||This note is for those of you with Automatic transmisions supported by only one mount under a large bracket bolted across the tranny. The problem is that unlike the motors with 2 tranny mounts, All the engine load is held by the soft bushings in the strut. The OEM strut isn't built to take this much load. <P>This weekend I needed to take apart the throttle body to clean it, and install a new IAC. When I took the TBI unit off the block I found that the seal between it and the manifold had been leaking signifigantly. The holes for the mounting bolts also show some damage.<P>Check your strut carefully, and check the air cleaner and trunk wall for signs of contact. Make sure your the engine end of your strut is bolted all the way to the back, trunk end, of the slot. (The engine spends most of its time trying to compress the strut.)<P>If the air cleaner still hits the trunk, replace the strut ASAP. Repeated pounding on the trunk wall can do major damage if let go. You could end up with a broken intake manifold. Your almost certain to end up with the gasket under the TBI leaking.<P>Even if you do have 2 tranny mounts, check the air cleaner just to be safe. Yours might not be able to hit the trunk as hard, but you should not let it hit at all if posible.<P>This problem could infact be part of why some people with 4 cyl motors, myself included, have had some really dificult to pin down performance problems. If the TBI is leaking at the base it can throw off the whole motor. if it's a small leak it can throw off just the IAC.....||||reg||1
Z||000001||88 Fiero||10-11-1999||04:32 PM||||That's a very good info Ogre...A peculiar thing when I was taking my '85GT apart and transferring it to my '88GT is that the struts fit perfectly...I didn't catch that till I got home from my friends shop...I always think that the struts are different from the '88 and '85..what do you think???||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-11-1999||08:24 PM||||I'm getting some mixed info about the V6. Couldn't say for sure if all struts are the same. It would seem to me they would be. All the 4 cyl ones are the same lenght no matter what type bushings are in it.<P>I believe that all V6 have 4 mounts plus the strut. The distance between the mounts has the effect of adding strut stiffness. The 4 cyl motor/tranny set with 3 mounts has much the same advantage. These setups are not as prone to the above problem.<P>Maybe I can explain this better,<P>Because the mass of the motor/tranny is centered between the mounts, the motor has to climb on top of the mounts to rotate. That adds several hundred pounds of damping to the drivetrain.<P>In the 86.5 4 Cyl & auto trans mounting that uses only 2 mounts to support the engine, the mass of the motor/tranny is directly over the 2 mounts. This arangement holds up the motor, but eliminates the nearly all the damping of the older setup. In this new arangement the strut takes all the torque.<P>To get an idea of how much diferance the mounts make. Take a bucket that's half full of water or sand. Hold it by the sides about 1/2 way down and walk. The bucket is verry stable. This is like the wide spaced mounts used on all the manual tranny setups. It takes very little additional stablizing even if you stir up the content of the bucket.<P>Now ballance the bucket on the edge of a 2X4. the bucket is not only hard to balance, but it falls over at the smallest push. This is like the pair of mounts used in the 86.5 automatic/4 cyl setups. This less stable setup takes far more force at the top of the "bucket" to keep it from falling over when you hit the gas. The problem is that GM didn't specify stiffer bushings in the strut for this setup.<P><BR>This problem is Not unique to only latter Fiero as far as I know. GM used this mount set in all 4 cyl/auto tranny setups after 86.5 thru sometime in the early 90's. If you own a Cavlier, Grand Am, 6000, Cutlass Ciera, etc, that have only the 2 motor/tranny mounts, check them to. If the bone in these cars is bad, they will likely break the bone or brackets. once that happens the power steering pump and AC compressor lines are going to be very short lived. I saw an 87 cutlass ciera recently where the car was effectivly ruined because the strut bracket broke from a bad strut that was ignored.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||10-12-1999||09:01 AM||||I feel like its probably time to replace the dogbone in my V6, and the obvious choice is the ultimate dog bone. However, I'd like to be able to use the replacement poly bushings.  My problem is that because of some apparent differences in the V6 bones, Im not sure if I will be able to use the bushing kit if I buy it.<P>My car is an 87.  Are there any opinions or sure fire ways for me to determine which solution is best.  I dont want to spend the extra money on an entire dog bone if I can spend half as much to rebuild existing dogbone and it be functionally the same solution.  <P>Everyones .02 is appreciated......  ||||reg||10
Z||000004||theogre||10-12-1999||09:10 AM||||visit the thread "installing dogbone" most of that is there.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shiner||10-12-1999||11:45 PM||||Hey, I got an almost new looking dogbone for my GT at the junkyard for $2.  Yep, $2.  It would be more than that just to SHIP the ultimate dog bone!  Check your local junk yard first, you will be amazed at what you find sometimes<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||10-13-1999||12:12 AM||||Yeah,Ogre,I seem to recall that when I looked at the mount situation on my old car that there was an opportunity for the motor trans to teeter off of the mounts without the strut.<BR>On my V6,I just took the die grinder to the web inside the strut,and squeezed in a couple of bump stops from other suspension projects;very firm,no discomfort though,I think the other mounts are all gooey from years of oil seepage.||||reg||1
